AB  - Cadmium (Cd) contamination in agricultural soils poses a significant risk to ecosystems and human health. This study evaluated the ability of Solanum nigrum L. to phytoaccumulate Cd at two vegetative stages (75 and 125 days), using Trichoderma spp. as a biostimulant. The experiment was conducted under a completely randomized design with three replicates. Three treatments were evaluated: T0 (control), T1 (1 cc/plant), and T2 (2 cc/plant), in soil contaminated with 17.87 g/kg of Cd. The results showed that T2 was the most effective treatment, achieving a 69.2 % reduction in soil Cd concentration and the highest Cd accumulation in leaf tissue (22.77 mg/kg). Likewise, root colonization by Trichoderma spp. in T2 reached 65 % and was associated with greater phytoremediation efficiency. These findings suggest the use of the T2 treatment as the best strategy for the sustainable recovery of cadmium contaminated soils.
