AB  - Bovine semen analysis is a fundamental tool for strengthening genetic conservation and reproductive improvement programs in populations adapted to tropical conditions. The objective was to analyze sperm kinetics and morphometry as indicators of elite sperm quality in Criollos bulls were selected based on phenotypic characteristics and health status, and semen samples were obtained via electroejaculation. Kinetic and morphometric parameters were analyzed using the Computer Assisted Sperm Analysis system (CASA). The values for CLV (74.69 μm.s-1), AVP (41.19 μm.s-1), RLV (26.44 μm.s-1), BCF (9.14 Hz), and ALH (3.04 μm) fall within ranges indicative of sperm motility and progression capabilities that favor the physiological processes involved in fertilization; the values for the LIN (35.22 %), STR (61.77 %), and WOB (52.63 %) indices indicate that the spermatozoa possess the capacity to migrate within the female reproductive tract. Meanwhile, the morphometric values (area 20.78 μm², length 6.49 μm, width 3.93 μm, and perimeter 16.63 μm) provide evidence of structural integrity and the stability of spermatogenesis processes. The differences observed compared to specialized breeds reflect adaptive characteristics specific to this population, resulting from its evolution under tropical conditions. These results serve as a scientific reference for reproductive evaluation, sire selection, and the establishment of germplasm banks. These findings strengthen strategies for the conservation and sustainable use of animal genetic resources, contributing to the preservation of genotypes that are adapted and resilient in the face of current production and climate challenges.
